<style|<tuple|article|number-long-article|british|cqft-notes>>

<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|A Constructive Introduction to Quantum Field
  Theory>|<doc-subtitle|Lecture 3: Stochastic observables, the Polchinski
  flow and forward\Ubackward equations>|<doc-author|<author-data|<author-name|M.
  Gubinelli>|<author-affiliation|Mathematical Institute, University of
  Oxford>>>|<doc-date|November 2026 <emdash> <em|draft>>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    We decompose the Gaussian free field along a scale parameter and take as
    primary objects the <em|stochastic observables>: martingales in the
    filtration of finite-resolution measurements. Interactions are introduced
    through the Polchinski equation for the effective potential. Its
    probabilistic counterpart is a forward\Ubackward stochastic differential
    equation (FBSDE) for the field seen at increasing resolution, whose
    effective force is a martingale. Writing the FBSDE in the coordinate
    given by the conditional mean of the terminal field removes its drift and
    integrates the transport part of the force flow exactly. In the gradient
    case the remaining equation for the force is the Wetterich equation for
    the effective average action, whose propagator is the conditional
    covariance. We then discuss the variational structure of the flow
    (Boué\UDupuis formula and Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman equation) in finite
    and infinite volume. The additional material covers Wick powers as
    observables in every dimension and their products (operator product
    expansion), the algebraic and perturbative structure of the flow
    (semigroup, flatness, counterterms for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>),
    and the Dyson\USchwinger equations at every scale. The treatment is
    formal: we compute as if all objects were smooth and integrable, and we
    indicate where the real difficulties lie.
  </abstract>>

