  <section*|Introduction>

  In Lectures 1\U2 a Euclidean field was a probability measure on
  <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>, i.e. a random
  distribution <math|\<varphi\>>, and local operators were functions of
  <math|\<varphi\>>. This viewpoint hides the scale-by-scale organisation of
  the Wilson\UKadanoff picture<nbsp><cite|WK74>, and forces us to work with
  singular objects from the start. Already for the GFF, the Wick power
  <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>>: is not a random distribution when
  <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d>, since its regularisations are not even
  tight (Proposition<nbsp><hlink|3.11|cqft_notes_L2.tm#prop:no-wick> of
  Lecture 2).

  In this lecture we index everything by a <em|resolution>
  <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>. The field observed at resolution
  <math|t> is a smooth function <math|\<varphi\><rsub|t>>, and the
  information available at resolution <math|t> is the
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> generated by
  <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<leq\>t>>. A physical quantity
  is then not a single random variable but a <em|consistent family> of
  finite-resolution estimates of it, i.e. a martingale. This is the notion of
  stochastic observable of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>, on which this lecture is based;
  see also<nbsp><cite|Pol84|BK87|Sal99|BBD24> for the renormalisation group
  side and<nbsp><cite|BG20|BG24> for the variational side.

  <paragraph|Structure.> Part I (ŸŸ4\U7) is the core of the lecture: the
  setting, the Polchinski equation and its FBSDE, the FBSDE in force
  coordinates and the Wetterich equation, and the variational structure. Part
  II (ŸŸ8\U10) is additional material, referred to from Part I where needed.

  <paragraph|What is formal.> Rigorous statements require a
  finite-dimensional setting: a finite lattice, or a Fourier cutoff on the
  torus, together with a finite terminal resolution <math|T>. There, all the
  identities below are theorems under mild growth assumptions on the
  interaction, and their proofs are the computations we give. The hard
  problems are the limits <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> (ultraviolet) and infinite
  volume (infrared), together with the locality of the limiting objects. We
  mark with <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> the statements that we only
  derive at this level.

  <paragraph|Conventions.> As in Lectures 1\U2:
  <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,
  <math|A=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>>, <math|C=A<rsup|-1>> with kernel <math|G>,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> the GFF, <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,g|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>f*g>
  for real functions. For a functional <math|F> on smooth fields,
  <math|D*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
  and <math|D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> are its first and second
  derivatives, <math|D<rsub|h>*F=<around|\<langle\>|D*F,h|\<rangle\>>>.
  <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>> are the Hermite polynomials of variance
  <math|c>, and <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q>> is the centred Gaussian measure with
  covariance <math|Q>.

  <assign|section-nr|3><section*|Part I. Main lecture>

  <section|Setting: covariance profiles and observables><label|sec:obs>

  <subsection|Covariance profiles>

  <\definition>
    <label|def:profile>A <em|covariance profile> is a family
    <math|<around|(|Q<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<in\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>> of
    bounded, symmetric, translation invariant operators on
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with <math|Q<rsub|0>=0>,
    <math|t\<mapsto\>Q<rsub|t>> smooth, <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|t>*Q<rsub|t>\<geq\>0>,
    <math|Q<rsub|t>\<to\>C> as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, and such that
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> and <math|Q<rsub|t>> have smooth kernels
    for every <math|t\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  </definition>

  Our standard example is the <em|heat profile>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heat-profile>Q<rsub|t>\<assign\>C<space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>,<space|2em><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=<frac|2|t<rsup|3>><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>,
  </equation>

  which smooths the Green function with the heat kernel at time
  <math|t<rsup|-2>>. On <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> the kernels are explicit,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heat-kernel><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x-y|)>=<frac|2*t<rsup|d-3>\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsup|2>/t<rsup|2>>|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|d/2>><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-t<rsup|2>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|2>/4>,
  </equation>

  so <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> is concentrated on distances
  <math|\<lesssim\>1/t>, and in Fourier space <math|Q<rsub|t>> retains the
  modes <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<lesssim\>t>: <math|t> is a <em|momentum
  scale>, or resolution. Other profiles are useful, even when they violate
  some smoothness requirement of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:profile>. A
  sharp Fourier cutoff is <math|<wide|Q|^><rsub|t><around|(|k|)>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||k|\|>\<leq\>t>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  which is not smooth in <math|t>. In a <em|finite range decomposition>,
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)>=0> for
  <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<geq\>1/t> (Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:finite-range>).
  We write

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|t>\<assign\>Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x,x|)>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|T,t>\<assign\>Q<rsub|T>-Q<rsub|t>*<space|1em><around|(|t\<leq\>T|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|d\<geq\>2>, <math|c<rsub|t>\<to\>\<infty\>> as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:ct>).

  Let <math|<around|(|W<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>> be a cylindrical Brownian
  motion on <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|W<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>*W<rsub|s><around|(|y|)>|]>=<around|(|t\<wedge\>s|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|x-y|)>>,
  and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scale-GFF>X<rsub|t>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:scale-GFF>For every <math|t\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|X<rsub|t>> is a smooth Gaussian field with law
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>, and <math|<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>
    is a continuous martingale with <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|X<around|(|x|)>,X<around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t>.
    As <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|X<rsub|t>\<to\>X<rsub|\<infty\>>> in
    <math|H<rsup|-s><rsub|<math-up|loc>>> for <math|s\<gtr\><frac|d|2>-1>,
    and <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
    The increments <math|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>> are independent of
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<sigma\><around|(|X<rsub|s>:s\<leq\>t|)>>,
    with law <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>>>.
  </proposition>

  So the GFF is the terminal value of a Gaussian martingale of smooth fields:
  the <em|field at resolution <math|t>> (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:multiscale>).
  The proof is a variant of Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.2|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:existence>
  of Lecture 2 and is left as Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:scale-GFF>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|multiscale_gff.png>|1par|||><label|fig:multiscale>>|The
  field at resolution <math|t>, <math|X<rsub|t>>, for the heat profile on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>> (<math|m=4>, mean subtracted for display), at
  <math|t=8,32,128> and at the lattice cutoff, all built from the same white
  noise. The martingale <math|X<rsub|t>> reveals finer and finer details,
  while the large scales are fixed at low resolution.>

  <subsection|Stochastic observables>

  Fix a filtration <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>, for
  the moment that of <math|X>, and write <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\>[<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|]>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|<cite|BCG26>><label|def:obs> An <em|observable> is a real or
    complex <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)>>-martingale
    <math|O=<around|(|O<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>:
    <math|O<rsub|s>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|O<rsub|t>|]>> for
    <math|s\<leq\>t>. Its <em|expectation> is
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|O|\<rangle\>>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><around|[|O<rsub|t>|]>>,
    which is independent of <math|t>. An <em|observable field> is a family
    <math|<around|(|O<around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-M\>>> of
    observables.
  </definition>

  Every integrable <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<infty\>>>-measurable random
  variable <math|Y> gives a <em|closed> observable
  <math|O<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|Y|]>>, with
  <math|O<rsub|t>\<to\>Y>. The point of the definition is that interesting
  observables need <em|not> be closed: a martingale may diverge as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> (for instance <math|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>> for
  <math|d\<geq\>2>), and still carry a perfectly meaningful family of
  finite-resolution estimates. The next lemma is elementary but basic.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Asymptotic uniqueness><label|lem:uniq> (a) If
    <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> are observables with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsup|1><rsub|t>-O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>,
    then <math|O<rsup|1>=O<rsup|2>>. (b) If <math|<around|(|N<rsub|t>|)>> is
    any adapted process and <math|O> an observable with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|N<rsub|t>-O<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>,
    then <math|O<rsub|s>=lim<rsub|t\<to\>\<infty\>>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|N<rsub|t>|]>> in <math|L<rsup|1>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    (a) <math|O<rsup|1><rsub|s>-O<rsup|2><rsub|s>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around|[|O<rsup|1><rsub|t>-O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|]>>
    for every <math|t\<geq\>s>, and conditional expectation is an
    <math|L<rsup|1>> contraction. (b) Likewise,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|s>-\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*N<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|t>-N<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>>.
  </proof>

  So an observable is determined by its asymptotic behaviour. A <em|germ> for
  <math|O> is a family of functionals <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|t>>
  on smooth fields such that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|t>-<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>.
  The observable field <math|O<around|(|x|)>> is <em|local> if it admits
  germs of the form

  <\equation*>
    <wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)><around|(|x|)>=<wide|\<cal-O\>|\<abovering\>><rsub|t><around*|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,\<nabla\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,\<ldots\>,\<nabla\><rsup|k>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Germs are not unique, and this freedom reflects the freedom of choosing
  counterterms. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:uniq>(b), a germ determines the
  observable.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The field at resolution <math|t>><label|ex:scale-GFF> (a) Prove
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:scale-GFF>. <em|Hint:> on the torus,
    write <math|X<rsub|t>=<big|sum><rsub|n><around|(|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|q|\<dot\>><rsub|n><around|(|s|)><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>B<rsup|n><rsub|s>|)>*e<rsub|n>>.
    (b) For the heat profile, check <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s=C>
    and <math|Q<rsub|t>=C\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>>, and derive
    <eqref|eq:heat-kernel>. (c) Show that
    <math|x\<mapsto\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>> is a.s. real analytic for the
    heat profile.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Divergence of <math|c<rsub|t>>><label|ex:ct> For the heat profile
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, show that
    <math|c<rsub|t>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>>*E<rsub|1>*<around|(|m<rsup|2>/t<rsup|2>|)>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
    <frac|t|m>+O<around|(|1|)>> for <math|d=2>, where <math|E<rsub|1>> is the
    exponential integral, and that <math|c<rsub|t>\<asymp\>t<rsup|d-2>> for
    <math|d\<geq\>3>.
  </exercise>

  <section|Interactions: the Polchinski equation and
  FBSDEs><label|sec:polchinski>

  <subsection|Effective potentials>

  Fix a finite volume <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> and a
  terminal resolution <math|T\<less\>\<infty\>>, which plays the role of an
  ultraviolet cutoff: the reference field
  <math|X<rsub|T>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>> is smooth. Let
  <math|V<rsub|T>> be an interaction, a functional of smooth fields bounded
  below. Our main example is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phi4-VT>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>><around*|[|\<lambda\>*H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|T>|)>+\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<space|0.17em>H<rsub|2>*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|T>|)>|]>\<mathd\>x,
  </equation>

  with a mass counterterm <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>> to be fixed in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:pert> (<math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>=0> for
  <math|d=2>). The interacting measure at cutoff <math|T> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:nuT>\<nu\><rsub|T><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
  </equation>

  <\definition>
    The <em|effective potential> at resolution <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>>
    is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:Vt>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<assign\><big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<varphi\>+\<zeta\>|)>><space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>.
    </equation>
  </definition>

  <math|V<rsub|t>> is the interaction felt by the field at resolution
  <math|t> once the fluctuations between <math|t> and <math|T> have been
  integrated out: it is the Wilson effective action. At <math|t=0>,
  <math|V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=-log Z<rsub|T>> is the free energy. Here is
  the first key fact.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Marginals><label|prop:marginals> Let <math|\<bbb-P\>> be defined
    by <math|\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\>/\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>>,
    where <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>> is the law of the Gaussian process
    <math|X>. Then <math|<math-up|Law><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|T>>,
    and for every <math|t\<leq\>T>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:nut><math-up|Law><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|t>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|t><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\>Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
    </equation>

    Moreover, the conditional law of <math|X<rsub|T>> given
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:localisation>\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|X<rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>><around|(|G|)>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>><big|int>G*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)>><space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Under <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>, <math|X<rsub|T>=X<rsub|t>+<around|(|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>|)>>
    with an independent increment of law <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>>>.
    Hence <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>|]>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>|]>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>|]>>.
    The conditional law follows in the same way.
  </proof>

  So at every resolution the field is again a Gibbs measure, with reference
  covariance <math|Q<rsub|t>> and potential <math|V<rsub|t>>. The
  measure-valued martingale <math|<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|X<rsub|t>>|)><rsub|t>>
  goes from <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> at <math|t=0> to
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|X<rsub|T>>> at <math|t=T>: it is a <em|stochastic
  localisation> of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> in the sense of
  Eldan<nbsp><cite|Eld23>, adapted to the covariance
  profile<nbsp><cite|BBD24|BCG26>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Polchinski equation<nbsp><cite|Pol84>><label|thm:polchinski>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> <math|V<rsub|t>> solves the backward
    Hamilton\UJacobi equation

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:polchinski>\<partial\><rsub|t>*V<rsub|t>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|D*V<rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D*V<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t>|)>,<space|2em>t\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>,
    </equation>

    with terminal condition <math|V<rsub|T>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    <math|u<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>=\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|1|2>*\<Delta\><rsub|Q<rsub|T,t>>>u<rsub|T>>
    is a Gaussian convolution, where <math|\<Delta\><rsub|Q>\<assign\><math-up|Tr><around|(|Q*D<rsup|2>|)>>.
    Since <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*Q<rsub|T,t>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>,
    it solves the backward heat equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*u<rsub|t>=-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*u<rsub|t>|)>>.
    Insert <math|D<rsup|2>*u=<around|(|D*V\<otimes\>D*V-D<rsup|2>*V|)>*u> and
    divide by <math|-u>.
  </proof>

  The nonlinear term generates new, generally nonlocal, interactions (trees),
  and the trace term contracts pairs of fields with the covariance of the
  shell <math|<around|[|t,t+\<mathd\>t|]>> (loops). The substitution
  <math|u=\<mathe\><rsup|-V>>, which linearises the equation, is a Cole\UHopf
  transform: we come back to this in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:burgers>,
  and in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:algebra>.

  <subsection|The Polchinski process>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|FBSDE for the interacting field><label|thm:fbsde>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> Under <math|\<bbb-P\>> the process
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<assign\>X<rsub|t>>, the <em|interacting field at
    resolution <math|t>>, solves

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:fbsde>\<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>F<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=-D*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,
    </equation>

    with <math|\<Phi\><rsub|0>=0> and a <math|\<bbb-P\>>-cylindrical Brownian
    motion <math|W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>>>. In particular the <em|effective force>
    <math|<around|(|F<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<leq\>T>> is a
    <math|\<bbb-P\>>-martingale: an observable field of the interacting
    theory.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The density process is <math|M<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around|[|\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\>/\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>\|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|]>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>>,
    by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals>. By Itô's formula and
    <eqref|eq:polchinski>, its drift vanishes and
    <math|\<mathd\>M<rsub|t>=-M<rsub|t><around|\<langle\>|D*V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>>.
    By Girsanov's theorem, <math|W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>=W<rsub|t>+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*D*V<rsub|s><around|(|X<rsub|s>|)>\<mathd\>s>
    is a <math|\<bbb-P\>>-Brownian motion, which is <eqref|eq:fbsde>.
    Differentiating <eqref|eq:Vt> gives <math|D*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>>*<around|(|D*V<rsub|T>|)>>,
    which is the second identity. Alternatively, Itô's formula for
    <math|D*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>, together with the
    derivative of <eqref|eq:polchinski>, shows that its drift vanishes. Here
    one uses that <math|D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t>> is symmetric.
  </proof>

  Read as an equation for an unknown pair <math|<around|(|\<Phi\>,F|)>>,
  <eqref|eq:fbsde> is a <em|forward\Ubackward> system<nbsp><cite|MY99>. The
  field is driven forward in resolution by a force that is determined
  backward, as the conditional expectation of a functional of the terminal
  field:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fbsde-system>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,<space|2em>t\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>.
  </equation>

  Here and below we drop the superscript and write <math|W> for the Brownian
  motion driving the interacting field. The Polchinski equation is not needed
  to state <eqref|eq:fbsde-system>, and <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>=<math-up|Law><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>
  is recovered from the solution. This is the starting point of FBSDE
  constructions of Euclidean fields (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:fbsde-flow>):
  one solves <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> with renormalised <math|V<rsub|T>>, with
  estimates uniform in <math|T> and in the volume, and takes the limit. The
  field coordinate <math|\<Phi\>> is <em|not> an observable, only a
  semimartingale. Its martingale part is the Gaussian noise, and the
  observables are built from it and from <math|F>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|fbsde_flow.png>|1par|||><label|fig:fbsde-flow>>|The
  forward\Ubackward structure of <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, heat profile, at resolutions
  <math|t<rsub|1>\<less\>t<rsub|2>\<less\>t<rsub|3>\<less\>T>, all built from
  the same noise. Top: the field gains the fluctuation of each shell,
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>, and reveals
  finer modes. Bottom: the effective force
  <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
  for the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> interaction, determined backward from the
  terminal interaction. It is shown to first order in <math|\<lambda\>>,
  where it is the Wick cube <math|-4*\<lambda\>*H<rsub|3>*<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>;c<rsub|t>|)>>,
  and each panel is in its own units.>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Closed versus local>At finite <math|T> the force is a closed
    observable, <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|F<rsub|T>|]>>. For
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>>, <math|F<rsub|T><around|(|x|)>=-4*\<lambda\>*H<rsub|3>*<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|T>|)>-2*\<gamma\><rsub|T>*H<rsub|1>*<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|T>|)>>
    is local. As <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> one expects the force to converge as
    an observable. In <math|d=2> the limit is closed,
    <math|F<rsub|\<infty\>>=-4*\<lambda\>:\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|3>>:.
    In <math|d=3> it is expected not to be. If it were,
    <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|F<rsub|\<infty\>>|]>> with
    <math|F<rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>L<rsup|1>>, the DS equation of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:DS> would pass to the limit
    (<math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>, then <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>), with
    <math|F<rsub|\<infty\>>> paired only with functionals of
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>. Conditioning on
    <math|\<sigma\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>> would then give a
    logarithmic derivative of the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> measure that
    is an integrable function of the field,
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|A*h,\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<rangle\>>-\<bbb-E\><around|[|F<rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|h|)>\|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>>.
    This is not expected: the regularised logarithmic derivatives do not
    converge in <math|L<rsup|2>><nbsp><cite|ALZ06>. The limiting force would
    then be an observable in the proper sense, a compatible family with no
    terminal representative. Locality of the limiting force is the crux of
    the construction<nbsp><cite|BCG26>.
  </remark>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|polchinski_0d.pdf>|1par|||><label|fig:polchinski-0d>>|A
  zero-dimensional Polchinski process (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:zero-dim>):
  <math|Q<rsub|t>=1-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>>, <math|T=5>,
  <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*x<rsup|4>-<frac|3|2>*x<rsup|2>>.
  Left: the effective force <math|F<rsub|t>=-V<rsub|t><rprime|'>> is almost
  linear at low resolution and develops the double-well structure as
  <math|t\<to\>T>. Centre: paths of the FBSDE <eqref|eq:fbsde>, which split
  between the two wells (stochastic localisation). Right: histogram of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> (<math|2\<cdot\>10<rsup|4>> paths) against the
  density of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>.>

  <subsection|Observables of the interacting theory>

  For a functional <math|G> of the terminal field, the observable
  <math|O<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t><around|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
  is <math|O<rsub|t>=G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Gt>G<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>><around|(|G|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>><big|int>G*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Formally, <math|G<rsub|t>> solves the backward Kolmogorov equation of the
  interacting dynamics,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:kolmogorov>\<partial\><rsub|t>*G<rsub|t>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*G<rsub|t>|)>-<around|\<langle\>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*D*V<rsub|t>,D*G<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>=0,<space|2em>G<rsub|T>=G,
  </equation>

  which is the Doob transform of the Gaussian heat equation by
  <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>> (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:doob>).
  Conversely, any solution of <eqref|eq:kolmogorov> yields an observable
  <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>; at finite <math|T> it is
  closed, but as <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> it need not be. The force is the
  case <math|G=-D*V<rsub|T>>, and is special because it also drives the
  dynamics.

  <paragraph|Tangent observables.> Let <math|V<rsub|T><rsup|\<theta\>>>
  depend on a coupling <math|\<theta\>>, and let
  <math|U<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*V<rsub|t><rsup|\<theta\>>>.
  Differentiating <eqref|eq:Vt> gives <math|U<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|t>*<around|[|\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*V<rsub|T><rsup|\<theta\>><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>,
  an observable, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:tangent>\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|T>><around|[|G|]>=-<math-up|Cov><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|T><rsup|\<theta\>>><around*|(|G,\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*V<rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|T>|)>.
  </equation>

  Thus the tangent observables, such as the running
  <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>: for <math|\<theta\>=\<lambda\>> (together with
  the derivative of the counterterm <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|2>>:),
  describe the response of the theory to a change of coupling. They are the
  observables conjugate to the coordinates of theory space.

  <subsection|The force and a Burgers equation><label|sec:burgers>

  Differentiating <eqref|eq:polchinski>, the effective force
  <math|F<rsub|t>=-D*V<rsub|t>>, now as a function of the field, solves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:burgers>\<partial\><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>=-D*F<rsub|t>*<around|[|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>|]>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*F<rsub|t>|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|T>=-D*V<rsub|T>,
  </equation>

  an infinite-dimensional, backward, viscous <em|Burgers equation>. The
  Cole\UHopf transform <math|F=D*log u> linearises it, which is the statement
  that <math|u=\<mathe\><rsup|-V>> solves a heat equation. Two remarks on
  <eqref|eq:burgers>:

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item>it uses only the force, which is a local quantity (<em|intensive>),
    while <math|V<rsub|t>> is <em|extensive>, of order of the volume. This
    matters in infinite volume (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:infinite-volume>);

    <item>the Burgers flows for different covariances commute, and the
    curvature vanishes, when <math|D*F<rsub|t>> is symmetric, i.e. when
    <math|F<rsub|t>> is a gradient<nbsp><cite|BCG26>. For a non-gradient
    force, as for the general Wilson\UItô diffusions of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>,
    the flows do not commute.
  </enumerate-roman>

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Quadratic interactions><label|ex:quadratic> Let
    <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>. (a) Show that
    <math|V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,\<kappa\>*<around|(|1+\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|T,t>|)><rsup|-1>*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>+<math-up|const>>
    solves <eqref|eq:polchinski>. (b) Show that <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> is
    Gaussian with covariance <math|<around|(|Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>+\<kappa\>|)><rsup|-1>>:
    for <math|Q<rsub|T>\<to\><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>> this
    is the GFF of mass <math|<around|(|m<rsup|2>+\<kappa\>|)><rsup|1/2>>. (c)
    Write <eqref|eq:fbsde> explicitly. It is a linear FBSDE. Solve it, and
    check that <math|F<rsub|t>> is a martingale. <em|Moral:> the mass can be
    put either into the covariance or into the force.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Zero dimensions><label|ex:zero-dim> Take <math|\<cal-M\>> a point,
    <math|Q<rsub|t>=q<around|(|t|)>> scalar, increasing,
    <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|x|)>=\<lambda\>*x<rsup|4>-a*x<rsup|2>> with
    <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|a\<geq\>0> (a double well if
    <math|a\<gtr\>0>; Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:polchinski-0d> has
    <math|\<lambda\>=<frac|1|2>>, <math|a=<frac|3|2>>). (a) Write
    <eqref|eq:polchinski> and solve it numerically for
    <math|q<around|(|t|)>=1-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>>. (b) Simulate
    <eqref|eq:fbsde> by first computing <math|V<rsub|t><rprime|'>> on a grid,
    and compare the histogram of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> with
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*x<rsup|4>+a*x<rsup|2>-x<rsup|2>/2*q<around|(|T|)>>>.
    (c) Compute <math|V<rsub|t>> to second order in <math|\<lambda\>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Doob transform><label|ex:doob> Show that <math|G<rsub|t>> in
    <eqref|eq:Gt> solves <eqref|eq:kolmogorov>, by writing
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>G<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|1|2>*\<Delta\><rsub|Q<rsub|T,t>>><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>>G|)>>.
    Prove <eqref|eq:tangent>.
  </exercise>

  <section|The FBSDE in force coordinates: the Wetterich
  equation><label|sec:wetterich>

  In this section the forward\Ubackward system <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> is the
  primary object. We look for the coordinates in which it is simplest. They
  remove the predictable part of the forward dynamics, and they integrate
  exactly the bilinear (tree) part of the force flow <eqref|eq:burgers>. In
  the gradient case the resulting equation for the force is the Wetterich
  equation for the effective average action<nbsp><cite|Wet93|BTW02>.
  Throughout,

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|t>\<assign\>Q<rsub|T>-Q<rsub|t>,<space|2em><wide|S|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>,<space|2em>S<rsub|T>=0,
  </equation*>

  is the covariance still unresolved at resolution <math|t>, and
  <math|<around|(|\<Phi\>,F|)>> solves <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> with a
  martingale force <math|F<rsub|t>=F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>,
  <math|\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>=\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>.

  <subsection|State and costate>

  The FBSDE is the stochastic Pontryagin system of the control problem of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD>. The controlled field
  <math|\<mathd\>X=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|u<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>W|)>>
  has Hamiltonian <math|<around|\<langle\>|p,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*u|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
  which does not depend on the state. By the stochastic maximum principle
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:pontryagin>), the adjoint process <math|p> has
  no drift, <math|\<mathd\>p<rsub|t>=\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>,
  with terminal value <math|p<rsub|T>=-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>,
  and the optimal control is <math|u<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*p<rsub|t>>.
  Thus

  <\padded-center>
    <em|the field <math|\<Phi\>> is the state, and the force <math|F> is the
    costate.>
  </padded-center>

  The function <math|F<rsub|t><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>> with
  <math|F<rsub|t>=F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> is the
  <em|decoupling field> of the FBSDE, and the Burgers equation
  <eqref|eq:burgers> is its quasilinear PDE;
  <math|\<Upsilon\><rsub|t>=D*F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>>
  is the martingale representation integrand of the costate. The two terms of
  <eqref|eq:burgers> have distinct origins. The transport term
  <math|-D*F*<around|[|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*F|]>> comes from the drift of the
  state, and generates the tree diagrams. The diffusion term
  <math|-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*D<rsup|2>*F|)>> is
  the Itô correction, and generates the loops.

  Without noise the state\Ucostate pair follows the Hamiltonian flow of the
  free Hamiltonian <math|h<rsub|s><around|(|p|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|p,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*p|\<rangle\>>>:
  <math|<wide|p|\<dot\>>=0> and <math|<wide|x|\<dot\>>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*p>.
  From <math|t> to <math|T> this flow is the linear shear

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:shear>\<Sigma\><rsub|t>:<around|(|x,p|)>\<longmapsto\><around|(|x+S<rsub|t>*p,<space|0.27em>p|)>.
  </equation>

  Its trajectories are the characteristics of the transport part of
  <eqref|eq:burgers> (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:characteristics>).

  <subsection|Removing the predictable drift>

  The drift <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>> of the state is
  predictable, and the costate is a martingale. Hence the drift that remains
  to be accumulated on <math|<around|[|t,T|]>> is predicted, at resolution
  <math|t>, by transporting the current state with the current costate along
  the free flow <eqref|eq:shear>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The forecast><label|prop:psi> <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> The
    conditional mean of the terminal field is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:psi>\<psi\><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>,
    </equation>

    i.e. <math|<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>,F<rsub|t>|)>=\<Sigma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>,F<rsub|t>|)>>.
    It is a martingale, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:psi-noise>\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>*\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>=<around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D*F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|)>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Integrate the forward equation from <math|t> to <math|T> and condition on
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>>: since <math|F> is a martingale,
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|T><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s>\<mathd\>s=S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>.
    For the differential, the drift <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>
    of <math|\<Phi\>> cancels against <math|<wide|S|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>,
    and <math|S<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>=S<rsub|t>*\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>.
  </proof>

  So <math|\<psi\>> is a closed observable, the <em|field observable>
  of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>. The field splits as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:lambda-split>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=\<psi\><rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>:
  </equation>

  a martingale, minus the drift still expected on the unresolved scales,
  which vanishes at <math|t=T>. The noise of <math|\<psi\>> in
  <eqref|eq:psi-noise> has two sources: the fresh fluctuation of the shell,
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>, and the
  revision of the forecast of the remaining drift,
  <math|S<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>>. The coupling between the
  forward and the backward equations, which was in the drift of
  <math|\<Phi\>>, now sits in the volatility of <math|\<psi\>>. The
  coordinate <math|\<psi\>> is the <em|interaction picture> with respect to
  the noiseless (classical) part of the dynamics.

  <subsection|The FBSDE in the forecast coordinate>

  Assume that the map <math|x\<mapsto\>\<psi\>=x+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>
  is invertible, and write its inverse as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coord>x=\<psi\>+S<rsub|t>*<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<assign\>-F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation>

  so that <math|F<rsub|t>=-<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>>:
  the costate is now a function of the forecast. Introduce the <em|response>
  and the <em|regulator>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:response>H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<assign\><around*|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+D*<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>\<assign\>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>,<space|2em><wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<geq\>0.
  </equation>

  Differentiating the two maps <math|x\<leftrightarrow\>\<psi\>> gives
  <math|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D*F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D*<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>|)><rsup|-1>=H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|FBSDE in force coordinates><label|thm:transformed>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> In the coordinates
    <math|<around|(|\<psi\>,<with|math-font|cal*|F>|)>>, the system
    <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> becomes

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:transformed-fbsde>\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|t>=H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around*|[|D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,
    </equation>

    with <math|\<psi\><rsub|0>=S<rsub|0>*F<rsub|0>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>>
    and <math|\<psi\><rsub|T>=\<Phi\><rsub|T>>. The forward equation has no
    drift, and its covariance is <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>\<assign\>H<rsub|t>*<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    The decoupling field <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>> solves the pure
    diffusion equation

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:transformed-flow>\<partial\><rsub|t>*<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t>+<tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em><math-up|Tr><around*|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|2>*<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t>|)>=0,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|T>=D*V<rsub|T>.
    </equation>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The forward equation is <eqref|eq:psi-noise> with
    <math|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D*F<rsub|t>=H<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>,
    and its covariance is

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*H<rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>=H<rsub|t>*<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>.
    </equation*>

    The backward equation is <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|F<rsub|T>|]>>
    with <math|\<psi\><rsub|T>=\<Phi\><rsub|T>>. Since
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>> is a
    martingale and <math|\<psi\>> has no drift, the drift in Itô's formula
    for <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>> is
    the left-hand side of <eqref|eq:transformed-flow>.
  </proof>

  Compare <eqref|eq:transformed-flow> with the Burgers equation
  <eqref|eq:burgers>: the transport term has been absorbed exactly by the
  change of coordinates, and only the Itô term is left. If it is dropped,
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t>=D*V<rsub|T>> does not move: all the
  tree diagrams are contained in the coordinate change <eqref|eq:coord>. The
  loops are generated only by the quadratic variation
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>> of the forecast, i.e. by the randomness of the
  noise and of the force revisions, and the nonlinearity has moved into the
  field dependence of <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>>. None of this uses a gradient
  structure or a Gaussian convolution: Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:transformed>
  holds for any FBSDE of the form <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> with a Markov
  martingale force, gradient or not, for which the coordinate change is
  invertible. For a general terminal force
  <math|F<rsub|T>=\<frak-f\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>, replace
  <math|D*V<rsub|T>> by <math|-\<frak-f\>> in <eqref|eq:transformed-fbsde>
  and <eqref|eq:transformed-flow>.

  <subsection|Conditional moments>

  For the Polchinski process the response has a direct meaning.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:H-cov><with|font-shape|right|(formal)> If
    <math|F<rsub|t>=-D*V<rsub|t>> then

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>\<assign\><math-up|Cov><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)>=S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>*S<rsub|t>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|H<rsub|t>\<gtr\>0> is symmetric,
    <math|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D*F<rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>
    is always invertible, and <math|H<rsub|t>\<leq\>S<rsub|t>> when
    <math|V<rsub|T>> is convex.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By <eqref|eq:localisation>, the conditional law
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>>> is the tilt of
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<phi\>|)>> by
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>>>; differentiating twice in
    <math|\<phi\>> gives <math|<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>>|)>=S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>*S<rsub|t>>.
    On the other hand <math|H<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D*F<rsub|t>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1>-S<rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t>>.
    The last claim is the Brascamp\ULieb inequality.
  </proof>

  So the forecast coordinates are the first two conditional moments of the
  stochastic localisation <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals>:
  <math|<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>,<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>|)>=<around|(|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>,<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:localisation>). They are tied by Itô's formula:
  since <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>\<otimes\>\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>=\<psi\><rsub|t>\<otimes\>\<psi\><rsub|t>+<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>
  is a martingale,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cov-dynamics>\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=\<cal-C\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t,<space|2em><math-up|dCov><rsub|t>=-<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\><around|(|<text|martingale>|)>.
  </equation>

  Conditional variance is resolved at the rate
  <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>,
  which for the Gaussian reference is <math|S<rsub|t>*<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|localisation.pdf>|1par|||><label|fig:localisation>>|Stochastic
  localisation for a two-component field:
  <math|Q<rsub|t>=<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>|)>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>>,
  <math|T=4>, <math|V<rsub|T>> a tilted double well, and the contours show
  the density of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>. One path of <eqref|eq:fbsde> up to
  resolution <math|t> (navy), with its terminal value <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>>
  (open circle). The forecast <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>
  (gold) is the mean of the conditional law
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>>: teal samples, and the ellipse
  is two standard deviations of <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>. At low
  resolution the conditional law covers both wells; as <math|t> grows it
  localises on one of them.>

  <subsection|The gradient sector: Wetterich's equation>

  Now let <math|F<rsub|t>=-D*V<rsub|t>>. The graph
  <math|<around|{|<around|(|x,-D*V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)>|}>> is a
  Lagrangian submanifold of the (field, force) phase space, and the shear
  <eqref|eq:shear> is symplectic because <math|S<rsub|t>> is symmetric. Its
  image is again Lagrangian. It is a graph over <math|\<psi\>>: the map
  <math|x\<mapsto\>x+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>> is <math|S<rsub|t>>
  times the gradient of <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>,
  which is strictly convex since its Hessian is
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><math-up|Cov><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<gtr\>0>
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:H-cov>), so the map is injective. Hence
  the image is the graph of a gradient:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-gradient><with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t>=D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>,<space|2em>H<rsub|t>=<around*|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><rsub|T>=V<rsub|T>.
  </equation>

  The generating function of this canonical transformation is the Moreau
  (infimal convolution) transform

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moreau>V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=inf<rsub|\<psi\>><around*|{|\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|x-\<psi\>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-\<psi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>|}>,
  </equation>

  whose optimality conditions <math|x=\<psi\>+S<rsub|t>*D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>,
  <math|D*V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>
  are <eqref|eq:coord>. It is well defined by the same convexity: up to the
  change of variable <math|j=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x>, it is a Legendre
  transform. We use <eqref|eq:moreau> to fix the additive constant in
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>. Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:shear> shows the
  construction in zero dimensions.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|shear.pdf>|1par|||><label|fig:shear>>|The
  zero-dimensional model of Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:polchinski-0d> at
  <math|t=1.2>. Left: the shear <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>> (teal arrows) maps
  the graph of the force <math|F<rsub|t>=-V<rsub|t><rprime|'>> (navy) to a
  graph over the forecast <math|\<psi\>> (gold), which is the graph of
  <math|-\<Gamma\><rsub|t><rprime|'>>. Right: the bare potential
  <math|V<rsub|T>>, the effective potential <math|V<rsub|t>> and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, each shifted to have minimum <math|0>.>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Wetterich equation><label|thm:wetterich>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> solves

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:wetterich-Gamma>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around*|(|<around*|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>-S<rsub|t>|)>|]>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><rsub|T>=V<rsub|T>.
    </equation>

    Equivalently, the <em|effective average action>
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<assign\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>
    solves, up to a field-independent term,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:wetterich>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around*|(|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>|]>.
    </equation>

    It interpolates between the bare action
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|T>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+V<rsub|T><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>
    (<math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|T>=\<infty\>>) and the 1PI effective
    action <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>> of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> (<math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|0>=0>).
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Integrate <eqref|eq:transformed-flow> in field space. With
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>=D*\<Gamma\>>, the derivative of
    <math|H=<around|(|S<rsup|-1>+D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>> in a
    direction <math|v> is <math|-H*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|3>*\<Gamma\><around|[|v|]>*<space|0.17em>H>,
    so by the total symmetry of <math|D<rsup|3>*\<Gamma\>>,

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|v><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t>|)>=-<math-up|Tr><around*|(|H<rsub|t>*<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|3>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|[|v|]>|)>=-<math-up|Tr><around*|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|2>*<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t>|)><around|[|v|]>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <eqref|eq:transformed-flow> reads
    <math|D*<around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t>|)>|)>=0>,
    and <eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma> holds up to a function of <math|t> alone.
    That this function is <math|-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t>|)>>
    with the normalisation <eqref|eq:moreau> follows from the Polchinski
    equation by the envelope theorem (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:envelope>).
    For <eqref|eq:wetterich>, note that <math|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>=D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>.
    For the endpoints: <math|S<rsub|T>=0>; and at <math|t=0>,
    <math|S<rsub|0>=Q<rsub|T>>, so <eqref|eq:moreau> identifies
    <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>>,
    up to a constant, with the cumulant generating function of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> at <math|Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*x>, whose Legendre
    transform is <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>>
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:1PI>).
  </proof>

  Thus the Wetterich equation is the gradient-integrated form of the force
  equation <eqref|eq:transformed-flow>, i.e. of the backward half of the
  FBSDE in forecast coordinates<nbsp><cite|Mor94|BCG26>. Every object in it
  is an observable of the Polchinski process:

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Wetterich>|<cell|FBSDE>>|<row|<cell|field
    argument <math|\<psi\>>>|<cell|forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>>, the
    state transported by <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>>>|<cell|minus
    the costate, <math|-F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>>>|<row|<cell|propagator
    <math|<around|(|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>>>|<cell|conditional
    covariance <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>>>|<row|<cell|regulator
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>>>|<cell|precision
    of the unresolved shell, minus that of the
    prior>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>>|<cell|<math|<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|[|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>|)>|]>>>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The flow of <math|\<Gamma\>> is driven by the difference between the
  interacting and the free conditional covariances, paired with the shell
  <math|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>. It is <em|one-loop
  exact>, with the full propagator inside the trace. In the Gaussian case
  <math|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> is constant and only the constant term
  of <math|\<Gamma\>> flows. For the heat profile with <math|T=\<infty\>>,
  <math|S<rsub|t>=C*<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>|)>> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exp-regulator><with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>=<frac|A|\<mathe\><rsup|A/t<rsup|2>>-1>,
  </equation>

  which behaves like a mass term <math|t<rsup|2>> on the modes
  <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<ll\>t> (for <math|m\<ll\>t>) and is exponentially
  small on the modes <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<gg\>t>: the exponential
  regulator of the functional renormalisation group
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:regulator>).

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|exp_regulator.pdf>|0.55par|||><label|fig:regulator>>|The
  regulator <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>> of the heat profile
  (<math|T=\<infty\>>, <math|m=1>): close to the mass term <math|t<rsup|2>>
  (dotted) on the modes <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<ll\>t>, exponentially small
  on the modes <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<gg\>t>.>

  <subsection|Variational meaning and the tree level>

  <paragraph|Constrained free energies.> <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> By
  the Gibbs variational principle applied to the conditional law
  <eqref|eq:localisation>, and the identity
  <math|<math-up|Ent><around|(|\<nu\>\|\<mu\><rsup|S>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-x|)>|)>=<math-up|Ent><around|(|\<nu\>\|\<mu\><rsup|S>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<psi\>|)>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x-\<psi\>,S<rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-\<psi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>>
  for <math|\<nu\>> of mean <math|\<psi\>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:constrained>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=inf
    <around*|{|<big|int>V<rsub|T><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\>+<math-up|Ent><around*|(|\<nu\><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<psi\>|)>|)>:<with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|int>\<varphi\>*<space|0.17em>\<nu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=\<psi\>|}>.>|\<nobracket\>>
  </equation>

  So <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, the effective average action up to the
  quadratic term <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
  is the free energy of the unresolved shell constrained to the forecast
  <math|\<psi\>>, and <eqref|eq:moreau> is the minimisation over the
  forecast. In the language of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD>:
  <math|V<rsub|t>> is the value function of the control problem, and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> is its Moreau transform, i.e. the value function
  expressed in the forecast coordinate, the interaction picture of the
  classical part of the dynamics.

  <paragraph|The tree level.> Dropping the Itô term in
  <eqref|eq:transformed-flow> gives <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=V<rsub|T>>, and
  then <eqref|eq:moreau> becomes the Hopf\ULax formula

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hopf-lax>V<rsup|<math-up|tree>><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=inf<rsub|y><around*|{|V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y-x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|y-x|)>|\<rangle\>>|}>,
  </equation>

  the solution of the noiseless Polchinski equation
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*V=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|D*V,<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*D*V|\<rangle\>>>.
  It is the saddle point of the Gaussian convolution <eqref|eq:Vt>. Its
  minimiser solves the classical field equation
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|y-x|)>+D*V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>=0> in
  the shell <math|<around|[|t,T|]>> with background <math|x>, and expanding
  it in <math|V<rsub|T>> produces exactly the tree diagrams.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Stochastic maximum principle><label|ex:pontryagin> (a) For the
    control problem <eqref|eq:BD>, perturb an optimal control
    <math|u\<to\>u+\<varepsilon\>*v> and show that
    <math|p<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
    satisfies <math|u<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*p<rsub|t>>.
    Identify <math|p> with the force <math|F> of <eqref|eq:fbsde>. (b) Show
    that <math|\<Upsilon\><rsub|t>=D*F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>>,
    and derive <eqref|eq:psi-noise>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Characteristics><label|ex:characteristics> (a) Show that the
    transport equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*F+D*F*<around|[|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*F|]>=0>
    is solved by <math|F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=F<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>>,
    <math|y=x+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>>, and that its
    characteristics are the trajectories of <eqref|eq:shear>. (b) Verify that
    <eqref|eq:hopf-lax> solves <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*V=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|D*V,<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*D*V|\<rangle\>>>,
    and that it is the limit <math|\<hbar\>\<to\>0> of <math|-\<hbar\>*log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|x+\<zeta\>|)>/\<hbar\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<hbar\>*S<rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>>.
    (c) For <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\>*y<rsup|4>> in zero
    dimensions, solve the equation for <math|y> as a power series in
    <math|\<lambda\>>, and identify the coefficients as tree graphs.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Conditional moments>(a) Prove <math|<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<phi\>><rsub|t>|)>=S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>*S<rsub|t>>.
    (b) Prove <eqref|eq:cov-dynamics>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|A symplectic shear>(a) Show that <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>>
    preserves <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<mathd\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>\<mathd\>p<rsub|i>>
    iff <math|S<rsub|t>> is symmetric. (b) Show that the image of the graph
    of <math|-D*V<rsub|t>> under <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>> is the graph of
    <math|-D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, with <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> given by
    <eqref|eq:moreau>. (c) For a non-gradient force, show that
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:transformed> still holds, but that
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|t>> need not be a gradient.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The constant><label|ex:envelope> Differentiate <eqref|eq:moreau>
    in <math|t> at fixed <math|x> (envelope theorem), and use
    <eqref|eq:polchinski> to show that the quadratic terms cancel and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|t>|)>>.
    Deduce <eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma> with the stated constant.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Gaussian check>For <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    show that <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+<math-up|const>>,
    and solve the FBSDE <eqref|eq:transformed-fbsde> explicitly. Compare with
    Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:quadratic>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|1PI effective action><label|ex:1PI> (a) Let
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|K><around|(|j|)>=log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|j,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>>.
    Show that <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>=<with|math-font|cal*|K><rsup|\<ast\>>>
    up to a constant, and that <math|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>
    is the inverse covariance of the tilted measure with mean <math|\<psi\>>.
    (b) Integrate <eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma> with
    <math|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> replaced by
    <math|D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|T>>, and obtain the one-loop formula
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>\<approx\>V<rsub|T>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>log
    <around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*D<rsup|2>*V<rsub|T>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Trees versus loops>Using <eqref|eq:moreau>, show that
    <math|V<rsub|t>=\<Gamma\><rsub|t>-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>,S<rsub|t>*D*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>+O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|3>|)>>
    for the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> interaction <eqref|eq:phi4-VT>. Deduce
    from Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:second-order> that, to order
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>>, <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> is obtained from
    <math|V<rsub|t>> by removing exactly the term <math|k=1>, the
    one-particle-reducible tree; the bubble (<math|k=2>), the sunset
    (<math|k=3>) and the vacuum term (<math|k=4>) are 1PI and remain. Where
    does the sunset come from in <eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma>?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Regulators>Derive <eqref|eq:exp-regulator>. Compute
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>> for the sharp Fourier profile, and
    for <math|<wide|Q|^><rsub|t><around|(|k|)>=<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>-<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>+t<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    (whose kernels are not smooth, but the formulas make sense). In the last
    case show that <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>=t<rsup|2>> when
    <math|T=\<infty\>>: a mass regulator.
  </exercise>

  <section|Variational structure><label|sec:variational>

  <subsection|Finite volume: the Boué\UDupuis formula>

  Let <math|\<bbb-H\><rsub|a>> be the set of progressively measurable drifts
  <math|u:<around|[|0,T|]>\<to\>L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  and set

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|t><around|(|u|)>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*u<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s,<space|2em>X<rsup|u>\<assign\>X+I<around|(|u|)>,
  </equation*>

  the field driven by the control <math|u> under the Gaussian reference
  <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Boué\UDupuis<nbsp><cite|BD98>; Polchinski as HJB><label|thm:BD>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:BD>-log \<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>|]>=V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=inf<rsub|u\<in\>\<bbb-H\><rsub|a>>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around*|[|V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s|]>.
    </equation>

    The infimum is attained at the feedback control
    <math|u<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|s>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*D*V<rsub|s><around|(|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>><rsub|s>|)>>,
    and <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>><rsub|T>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|T>>.
    The effective potential is the value function, and <eqref|eq:polchinski>
    is the Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman equation of this control problem.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (Verification.) For any <math|u>, by Itô's formula and
    <eqref|eq:polchinski>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|t>|)>>|<cell|=<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2>\<\|\|\><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*D*V<rsub|t>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+<around|\<langle\>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*D*V<rsub|t>,u<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>M<rsub|t>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*D*V<rsub|t>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>-<tfrac|1|2>|\<\|\|\>>*u<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|]>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>M<rsub|t>,>>>>
    </align*>

    with <math|D*V<rsub|t>=D*V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|t>|)>> and a
    martingale <math|M>. Integrating and taking expectations,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:verification>\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around*|[|V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>t|]>=V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>+<frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*D*V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|t>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>t.
    </equation>

    So the cost is <math|\<geq\>V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>>, with equality iff
    <math|u=u<rsup|\<ast\>>>. With this control
    <math|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>>> solves the same SDE as <math|\<Phi\>> in
    <eqref|eq:fbsde>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Euler\ULagrange equation <math|=> FBSDE.> Perturbing
  <math|u\<to\>u+\<varepsilon\>*v> in <eqref|eq:BD>, the first-order
  condition is <math|\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<langle\>|u<rsub|s>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around|[|D*V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>|]>,v<rsub|s>|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>s=0>
  for all adapted <math|v>, i.e.

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:EL>u<rsub|s>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around*|[|D*V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>|]>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*F<rsub|s>.
  </equation>

  This is <eqref|eq:fbsde-system>. The FBSDE is the Euler\ULagrange equation
  of the variational problem, and the Polchinski equation is its
  Hamilton\UJacobi equation. These are the three faces of the same object:
  <em|the PDE> (Polchinski), <em|the stochastic dynamics> (FBSDE), and
  <em|the variational principle> (Boué\UDupuis).

  <paragraph|Entropy.> By Girsanov, <math|<frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<big|int><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geq\><math-up|Ent><around|(|<math-up|Law><around|(|X<rsup|u>|)><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em><math-up|Law><around|(|X|)>|)>>,
  which is at least the entropy of the terminal marginal. So <eqref|eq:BD> is
  a pathwise lift of the Gibbs variational principle <math|-log
  Z<rsub|T>=inf<rsub|\<nu\>><around|{|<big|int>V<rsub|T><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\>+<math-up|Ent><around|(|\<nu\>\|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>|)>|}>>.
  The lift is useful because adapted drifts can be <em|constructed scale by
  scale>: one looks for <math|u> as a functional of the Gaussian process
  <math|X>, not as an unknown measure.

  <paragraph|Generating functionals.> Adding a test functional <math|f>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:generating>\<cal-W\><rsub|T><around|(|f|)>\<assign\>-log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-f>\<mathd\>\<nu\><rsub|T>=inf<rsub|u>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around*|[|f<around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s|]>-V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation>

  Bounds on <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|T><around|(|f|)>> uniform in <math|T>, for
  <math|f> in a rich class, give tightness, moment bounds and the
  identification of limits. For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the naive
  problem does not have uniform bounds: the leading-order optimal drift
  <math|u<rsub|s>\<approx\>-4*\<lambda\>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>:X<rsub|s><rsup|3>>:
  has a divergent cost. One renormalises by the change of variables
  <math|u=-4*\<lambda\>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>:X<rsup|3>:+r>. After it,
  and after further paracontrolled changes of variables, the singular terms
  are explicit polynomials in the Wick powers of <math|X>, cancelled by
  <em|the counterterms>, and the residual functional is coercive. Its control
  in Besov and paracontrolled norms, uniformly in <math|T>, is the
  construction of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> in<nbsp><cite|BG20>; see
  also<nbsp><cite|BG21b>.

  <subsection|Infinite volume><label|sec:infinite-volume>

  On <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> the free energy
  <math|V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>> and the effective potential <math|V<rsub|t>>
  are extensive: they grow like <math|L<rsup|d>>, and <eqref|eq:BD> has no
  limit as <math|L\<to\>\<infty\>>. Three objects are intensive and survive
  formally:

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item><em|the force.> For a local interaction
    <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|int>v<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<mathd\>x>,
    <math|D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)><around|(|x|)>=v<rsub|T><rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|)>>,
    and <eqref|eq:fbsde-system> reads, on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:fbsde-inf>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=-\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|v<rsub|T><rprime|'><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><around|(|x|)>|)>|]>.
    </equation>

    Here every quantity is local, and the equation makes sense without a
    volume cutoff. Infinite-volume limits of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|T><rsup|<around|(|L|)>>> are expected to be laws of
    solutions of <eqref|eq:fbsde-inf>. Uniqueness of such solutions (e.g. for
    small coupling, or by contraction in weighted spaces) is a uniqueness
    statement for the infinite-volume theory. Non-uniqueness is expected in
    the presence of phase transitions, e.g. for
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> with a deep double-well
    potential<nbsp><cite|GJS75>;

    <item><em|the Burgers flow> <eqref|eq:burgers> of the force, for the same
    reason;

    <item><em|differences of free energies.> For a local <math|f>,
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|T><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|f|)>> in
    <eqref|eq:generating> is a difference of two extensive infima, and it
    stays bounded as <math|L\<to\>\<infty\>>. Its variational
    characterisation, through the infinite-volume Euler\ULagrange equation
    <eqref|eq:fbsde-inf>, is the basis of the infinite-volume analysis of
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> and <math|exp
    <around|(|\<Phi\>|)><rsub|2>> in<nbsp><cite|BG24>.
  </enumerate-roman>

  The coercive estimates must then be <em|local>: one uses weighted norms and
  needs bounds whose constants do not depend on the volume. This infrared
  problem is the analogue, for the scale dynamics, of the \Pcoming down from
  infinity\Q estimates for the parabolic dynamics.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Optimal drift>(a) Derive <eqref|eq:EL> from <eqref|eq:BD>, and
    check that the optimal <math|u> is a martingale up to the factor
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>>. (b) For the quadratic interaction of
    Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:quadratic>, compute the optimal drift and the
    value <eqref|eq:BD> explicitly.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Cost of the leading drift>For <math|V<rsub|T>=\<lambda\>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:<rsub|T>>
    on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3><rsub|L>>, show that

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T>\<\|\|\>4*\<lambda\>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>:X<rsub|s><rsup|3>:\<\|\|\><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s=12*\<lambda\><rsup|2>*L<rsup|3>*<big|int>Q<rsub|T><around|(|z|)><rsup|4><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z,
    </equation*>

    which diverges linearly in <math|T>. Relate this to the <math|k=4> term
    of <eqref|eq:second-order>.
  </exercise>

  <section*|Part II. Additional material>

  The following sections complement the main lecture. They develop the
  observables of the Gaussian free field and their products, the algebraic
  and perturbative structure of the Polchinski flow, and the Dyson\USchwinger
  equations at every scale.

  <section|Observables of the Gaussian free field and their
  products><label|sec:gff-obs>

  <subsection|Wick powers and exponentials>

  <paragraph|Point evaluations.> For every <math|x> and every <math|d>,
  <math|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>\<assign\><around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>
  is a local observable. For <math|d\<geq\>2> it is not closed:
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>=c<rsub|t>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  <paragraph|Wick powers.> Recall that <math|\<partial\><rsub|c>*H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>=-<frac|1|2>*\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|2>*H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>>
  and <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*H<rsub|n>=n*H<rsub|n-1>> (differentiate the
  generating function <math|\<mathe\><rsup|\<tau\>*a-c*\<tau\><rsup|2>/2>>).

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Wick observables><label|prop:wick-obs> For every <math|d\<geq\>1>,
    <math|n\<geq\>1> and <math|x\<in\>\<cal-M\>>,

    <\equation*>
      :X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>
    </equation*>

    is a continuous martingale, with <math|\<mathd\><space|0.17em>:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>=n<space|0.17em>:X<rsup|n-1><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>
    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:wick-obs-cov>\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>:X<rsup|k><rsub|t>:<around|(|y|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)><rsup|n>.
    </equation>

    In particular, for <math|x\<neq\>y>, <math|lim<rsub|t>
    \<bbb-E\>[:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|y|)>]=n!<space|0.17em>G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By Itô's formula, using <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|X<around|(|x|)>,X<around|(|x|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=<wide|c|\<dot\>><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t>,

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*H<rsub|n><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>+<around*|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|2>*H<rsub|n>+\<partial\><rsub|c>*H<rsub|n>|)>*<wide|c|\<dot\>><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t=n*H<rsub|n-1>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The covariance is Lemma<nbsp><hlink|3.8|cqft_notes_L2.tm#lem:hermite> of
    Lecture 2, applied to the Gaussian vector
    <math|<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,X<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>|)>>.
  </proof>

  Compare with Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.9|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:wick> of Lecture
  2: there <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>>: existed as a random distribution only
  for <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<less\>d>, and by
  Proposition<nbsp><hlink|3.11|cqft_notes_L2.tm#prop:no-wick> not at all
  otherwise. As observables, Wick powers exist in <em|every> dimension and
  need no smearing. When <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d> and
  <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0>, the smeared martingale
  <math|t\<mapsto\>:X<rsub|t><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>> is unbounded in
  <math|L<rsup|2>> and, by the Paley\UZygmund argument of Lecture 2, not even
  tight as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>: it has no terminal value, and the
  observable is the whole family, not a limit. The singularity has not
  disappeared. It has moved from the observables themselves to their
  <em|products> at coincident points, as we now see.

  <paragraph|Exponentials.> For <math|\<beta\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>,
  <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\>*X<rsub|t>>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>exp
  <around|(|\<beta\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>-<frac|\<beta\><rsup|2>|2>*c<rsub|t>|)>>
  is the stochastic exponential of <math|\<beta\>*X<around|(|x|)>>, hence a
  martingale, and <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|1>*X<rsub|t>>:<around|(|x|)>:\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|2>*X<rsub|t>>:<around|(|y|)>]=\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|1>*\<beta\><rsub|2>*Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)>>>.

  <subsection|Products of observables><label|sec:product>

  The pointwise product of two martingales is not a martingale. The natural
  definition of the product keeps the asymptotics.

  <\definition>
    <label|def:product>Two observables <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> <em|admit a
    product> if <math|O<rsup|1><rsub|t> O<rsup|2><rsub|t>\<in\>L<rsup|1>> for
    all <math|t> and there is an observable <math|O<rsup|1>
    \<star\>*O<rsup|2>> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|O<rsup|1>
    \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|)><rsub|t>-O<rsup|1><rsub|t>
    O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:uniq> it is unique, and
    <math|<around|(|O<rsup|1> \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|)><rsub|s>=lim<rsub|t>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|O<rsup|1><rsub|t> O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|]>>.
  </definition>

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:product>Let <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> be continuous
    observables, square integrable on bounded intervals, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:prod-cond>\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|s>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation>

    Then <math|O<rsup|1> \<star\>*O<rsup|2>> exists and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:prod-formula><around|(|O<rsup|1>
      \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|)><rsub|t>=O<rsup|1><rsub|t>
      O<rsup|2><rsub|t>+\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|s>|]>.
    </equation>

    In particular <math|<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>
    \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>=O<rsup|1><rsub|0>
    O<rsup|2><rsub|0>+\<bbb-E\>*<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <math|P<rsub|t>\<assign\>O<rsup|1><rsub|t>
    O<rsup|2><rsub|t>-<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>>
    is a martingale on bounded intervals, and
    <math|N<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    is a closed martingale by <eqref|eq:prod-cond>. So <math|P+N> is a
    martingale and equals the right-hand side of <eqref|eq:prod-formula>. Its
    distance from <math|O<rsup|1><rsub|t> O<rsup|2><rsub|t>> in
    <math|L<rsup|1>> is at most <math|\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<to\>0>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Products of point evaluations.> Here
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>,<math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
  is deterministic and <eqref|eq:prod-cond> reads
  <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>|\|>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  For the heat profile and <math|d\<geq\>2> this holds iff <math|x\<neq\>y>,
  and then <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>\<geq\>0> and the integral is
  <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)>>. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:XX><around|(|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|t>=X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>*X<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>+G*<around|(|x-y|)>-Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x-y|)>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>>=G*<around|(|x-y|)>,
  </equation>

  which is the correct two-point function. We never had to discuss the
  regularity of <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>>>. For <math|x=y> and <math|d\<geq\>2>
  the product does not exist: its expectation would be <math|lim<rsub|t>
  \<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>=lim<rsub|t> c<rsub|t>=\<infty\>>.

  <paragraph|Operator product expansion.> Formula <eqref|eq:XX> has an exact
  short-distance expansion at every resolution: for fixed <math|t>, as
  <math|y\<to\>x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:OPE><around|(|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|t>-G*<around|(|x-y|)><space|0.27em>\<longrightarrow\><space|0.27em>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>-c<rsub|t>=:X<rsup|2><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  Since both sides are martingales, this reads
  <math|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>=G*<around|(|x-y|)>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+:<math-bf|X><rsup|2>:<around|(|x|)>+o<around|(|1|)>>,
  the remainder tending to zero at each fixed resolution <math|t>: the Wick
  square is the regular part of the product, and <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)>> is
  its singular coefficient. More generally
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:wick-product>), for <math|x\<neq\>y>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:wick-product>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>*\<star\>:<math-bf|X><rsup|k>:<around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<leq\>n\<wedge\>k>j!<binom|n|j><binom|k|j><space|0.17em>G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|j><space|0.17em>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n-j><around|(|x|)><math-bf|X><rsup|k-j><around|(|y|)>:,
  </equation>

  where <math|:<math-bf|X><rsup|p><around|(|x|)><math-bf|X><rsup|q><around|(|y|)>:<rsub|t>\<assign\>H<rsub|p>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>*H<rsub|q>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>>
  minus its contractions at scales <math|\<leq\>t>. This is Wick's theorem
  written at the level of observables. The coefficients
  <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|j>> carry all the singularities, and the
  observables on the right have regular limits as <math|y\<to\>x>.

  <paragraph|Locality and factorisation.> Let <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> be
  observables with germs supported in <math|U<rsup|1><rsub|1/t>> and
  <math|U<rsup|2><rsub|1/t>>, where <math|U<rsup|i>> are sets at positive
  distance and <math|U<rsub|r>> is the <math|r>-neighbourhood of <math|U>.
  The covariation <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=<around|\<langle\>|D*O<rsup|1><rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D*O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>t>
  involves <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> only between the two supports. For
  a finite range decomposition it vanishes for large <math|t>, so the product
  exists and is asymptotically the pointwise product. For the heat profile it
  is exponentially small, by <eqref|eq:heat-kernel>. Thus, under
  integrability conditions on the drifts of the germs, observables supported
  on disjoint regions can be multiplied, while the products of observables at
  the same point require renormalisation (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:locality>).
  This is the structure of a <em|factorisation algebra> of observables; the
  precise axioms are not established here<nbsp><cite|BCG26>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|locality.png>|1par|||><label|fig:locality>>|Covariation
  of point observables, heat profile in <math|d=2>. Left: the kernels of
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>> centred at <math|x> (navy) and at
  <math|y> (teal) at three resolutions, with their overlap (gold). The
  integral of the product of the two kernels is the covariation density
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>>. Right:
  <math|s\<mapsto\><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>> for three
  distances (<math|m=1>). Its integral is <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)>>, and it
  is negligible for <math|s\<gg\>1/<around|\||x-y|\|>>. As <math|y\<to\>x>
  the mass moves to <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>, which is the divergence of the
  product at coincident points.>

  <\remark>
    <label|rem:finite-range>A finite range decomposition makes these
    statements exact: if <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)>=0>
    for <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<geq\>1/t>, then <math|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>>
    restricted to regions at distance <math|\<geq\>1/t> are independent. Such
    decompositions of <math|<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>> exist
    and are a basic tool of the rigorous renormalisation
    group<nbsp><cite|BBS19>.
  </remark>

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Hermite calculus along the flow><label|ex:hermite-flow> (a) Show
    that <math|<big|int>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a+b;c|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|s><around|(|\<mathd\>b|)>=H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c-s|)>>
    for <math|0\<leq\>s\<leq\>c>, where <math|\<mu\><rsup|s>=N<around|(|0,s|)>>.
    (b) Deduce again that <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>>
    is a martingale, using only the independence of the increments.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Products of Wick observables><label|ex:wick-product> (a) Show that
    <eqref|eq:prod-cond> holds for <math|:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>>
    and <math|:<math-bf|X><rsup|k>:<around|(|y|)>> when <math|x\<neq\>y> and
    <math|Q> is the heat profile. <em|Hint:> <math|c<rsub|s>> grows at most
    polynomially while <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
    decays like <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-s<rsup|2>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|2>/4>>.
    (b) Show that <math|\<langle\>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>*\<star\>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|y|)>\<rangle\>=n<rsup|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|n-1|)>!<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|s><rsup|n-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s=n!<space|0.17em>G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n>>.
    (c) Prove <eqref|eq:wick-product>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Exponential observables>For <math|\<beta\>=\<mathi\>b>,
    <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, and the heat profile, show that condition
    <eqref|eq:prod-cond> for <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>b<math-bf|X>>:<around|(|x|)>*\<star\><wide|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>b<math-bf|X>>:<around|(|y|)>|\<bar\>>>
    reads <math|<big|int><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|b<rsup|2>*c<rsub|s>><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Deduce that the product exists for all <math|x\<neq\>y> if
    <math|d\<leq\>3>, for <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<gtr\><around|\||b|\|>/2*\<pi\>>
    but not for <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<less\><around|\||b|\|>/2*\<pi\>> if
    <math|d=4>, and not at all if <math|d\<geq\>5>. For <math|d\<leq\>3> show
    that its expectation is <math|\<mathe\><rsup|b<rsup|2>*G*<around|(|x-y|)>>>,
    and decide for which <math|b> it is locally integrable in <math|y>: all
    <math|b> in <math|d=1>, <math|b<rsup|2>\<less\>4*\<pi\>> in <math|d=2>
    (the <math|L<rsup|2>> threshold of Lecture 2), only <math|b=0> in
    <math|d=3>.
  </exercise>

  <section|Algebraic structure of the Polchinski flow><label|sec:algebra>

  <subsection|A semigroup of renormalisation maps>

  For a positive covariance <math|S> define the renormalisation map

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:RG-map>\<cal-R\><rsub|S><around|(|V|)>\<assign\>-log
    <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|S>\<ast\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|\<mu\><rsup|S>\<ast\>u|)><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=<big|int>u*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)>*\<mu\><rsup|S><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Since <math|\<mu\><rsup|S>\<ast\>\<mu\><rsup|S<rprime|'>>=\<mu\><rsup|S+S<rprime|'>>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:semigroup>\<cal-R\><rsub|S>\<circ\>\<cal-R\><rsub|S<rprime|'>>=\<cal-R\><rsub|S+S<rprime|'>>=\<cal-R\><rsub|S<rprime|'>>\<circ\>\<cal-R\><rsub|S>,<space|2em>V<rsub|t>=\<cal-R\><rsub|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|V<rsub|T>|)>.
  </equation>

  So the effective potentials are an orbit of an <em|abelian> semigroup,
  indexed by the cone of positive covariances, acting nonlinearly on
  interactions and linearly on Boltzmann factors <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V>>.
  Its infinitesimal generators are the vector fields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:PB>\<cal-P\><rsub|B><around|(|V|)>\<assign\><tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|B*D<rsup|2>*V|)>-<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|D*V,B*D*V|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><tfrac|\<mathd\>|\<mathd\>s>\<cal-R\><rsub|s*B><around|(|V|)><mid|\|><rsub|s=0>=\<cal-P\><rsub|B><around|(|V|)>,
  </equation>

  and <eqref|eq:polchinski> reads <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*V<rsub|t>=-\<cal-P\><rsub|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>><around|(|V<rsub|t>|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Flatness><label|prop:flat> <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> (a)
    <math|<around|[|\<cal-P\><rsub|B>,\<cal-P\><rsub|B<rprime|'>>|]>=0> for
    all positive <math|B,B<rprime|'>>. (b) <math|V<rsub|t>> depends on the
    profile only through <math|Q<rsub|T,t>>. In particular the free energy
    <math|V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>> and the law <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> depend
    only on <math|Q<rsub|T>>, not on the path <math|t\<mapsto\>Q<rsub|t>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    (a) Differentiate <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|s*B>*\<cal-R\><rsub|r*B<rprime|'>>=\<cal-R\><rsub|r*B<rprime|'>>*\<cal-R\><rsub|s*B>>
    at <math|s=r=0>, or compute directly (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:commutator>).
    (b) is <eqref|eq:semigroup>.
  </proof>

  Different profiles with the same endpoints are different <em|coordinate
  systems> on the same renormalisation group trajectory. They change the
  process <math|\<Phi\>> and the observables at intermediate resolution, but
  not the terminal theory. The flow has no holonomy<nbsp><cite|BCG26>.

  <subsection|Perturbation theory and counterterms><label|sec:pert>

  Expanding <math|V<rsub|t>=\<cal-R\><rsub|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|V<rsub|T>|)>>
  in cumulants of the Gaussian increment <math|\<zeta\>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>>>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cumulants>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<zeta\>>*<around*|[|V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)>|]>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Var><rsub|\<zeta\>><around*|(|V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)>|)>+\<ldots\>
  </equation>

  For <eqref|eq:phi4-VT>, Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:hermite-flow> shows
  that Gaussian convolution simply <em|lowers the Wick ordering>: since
  <math|Q<rsub|T,t>*<around|(|x,x|)>=c<rsub|T>-c<rsub|t>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:first-order>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=<big|int><around*|[|\<lambda\>*H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>+\<gamma\><rsub|T>*H<rsub|2>*<around|(|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>|]>\<mathd\>x+O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|)>> is kept for
  later use. At first order the effective potential is the Wick-ordered
  potential at the current resolution. This is the dual statement of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wick-obs>:
  <math|<big|int>H<rsub|4>*<around|(|X<rsub|t>;c<rsub|t>|)>> is an
  observable.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Second order><label|prop:second-order>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> Write <math|S=Q<rsub|T,t>> and
    <math|:\<phi\><rsup|p>:<rsub|t>=H<rsub|p>*<around|(|\<phi\>;c<rsub|t>|)>>.
    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:second-order>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=<big|int><around*|[|\<lambda\>:\<phi\><rsup|4>:<rsub|t>+\<gamma\><rsub|T>:\<phi\><rsup|2>:<rsub|t>|]>-<frac|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|4><binom|4|k><rsup|2>k!<big|iint>S*<around|(|x,y|)><rsup|k><space|0.17em>:\<phi\><rsup|4-k>:<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>:\<phi\><rsup|4-k>:<rsub|t><around|(|y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>y+O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|3>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By the binomial formula for Hermite polynomials,

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>;c<rsub|t>+s|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|4><binom|4|k>H<rsub|k>*<around|(|\<zeta\>;s|)>*H<rsub|4-k>*<around|(|\<phi\>;c<rsub|t>|)>,<space|2em>s=S*<around|(|x,x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then compute the variance in <eqref|eq:cumulants> with
    Lemma<nbsp><hlink|3.8|cqft_notes_L2.tm#lem:hermite> of Lecture 2.
  </proof>

  Now let <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> at fixed <math|t>, so that
  <math|S\<to\>C-Q<rsub|t>>, which near the diagonal is as singular as
  <math|G>. In <math|d=2> every <math|S<rsup|k>> is locally integrable, so
  with <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>=0> every term has a limit: Wick ordering
  suffices. In <math|d=3>, <math|G<around|(|z|)>\<sim\><around|(|4*\<pi\><around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|-1>>:

  <\itemize>
    <item><math|k=1,2>: <math|S> and <math|S<rsup|2>> are locally integrable.
    These terms are finite and nonlocal.

    <item><math|k=3> (the <em|sunset>): <math|S<rsup|3>\<sim\><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-3>>
    is not integrable. Writing <math|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>*\<phi\><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<phi\><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>+\<phi\><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>|)>-<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>-\<phi\><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|2>>,
    up to a field-independent constant the divergent part is local:
    <math|-48*\<lambda\><rsup|2><around*|(|<big|int>S<rsup|3>|)>*<big|int>:\<phi\><rsup|2>:<rsub|t>+>(finite),
    with <math|<big|int>S<rsup|3>\<sim\><frac|1|16*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log T>. It
    is cancelled by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:gammaT>\<gamma\><rsub|T>\<assign\>48*\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3><rsub|L>>Q<rsub|T><around|(|z|)><rsup|3><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z=<frac|3*\<lambda\><rsup|2>|\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
      T+O<around|(|1|)>,
    </equation>

    since <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>-48*\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<big|int>S<rsup|3>>
    converges (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:sunset>).

    <item><math|k=4>: a divergent constant
    <math|-12*\<lambda\><rsup|2>*L<rsup|3>*<big|int>S<rsup|4>\<sim\>T>, the
    vacuum energy. It cancels in <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> but not in the free
    energy.
  </itemize>

  Power counting (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:power-counting>) shows that no
  further field-dependent counterterms are needed in <math|d=3>:
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> is superrenormalisable. Thus renormalisation
  appears as a property of the flow: one chooses the terminal data
  <math|V<rsub|T>> so that the effective potentials <math|V<rsub|t>> at
  <em|fixed> resolution have limits. The divergences at second order are also
  related to the singularity of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> with respect
  to the GFF<nbsp><cite|BG21b>: the measures <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> at fixed
  <math|t> are expected to stay absolutely continuous with respect to
  <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>, while the terminal one is not.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Commuting flows><label|ex:commutator> Show by direct computation
    that <math|D*\<cal-P\><rsub|B><around|(|V|)><around|[|\<cal-P\><rsub|B<rprime|'>><around|(|V|)>|]>=D*\<cal-P\><rsub|B<rprime|'>><around|(|V|)><around|[|\<cal-P\><rsub|B><around|(|V|)>|]>>.
    Which property of <math|D<rsup|2>*V> and <math|D<rsup|3>*V> is used?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The sunset><label|ex:sunset> (a) Show that
    <math|<big|int><rsub|<around|\||z|\|>\<leq\>1>G<around|(|z|)><rsup|3><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z=\<infty\>>
    in <math|d=3>, and that <math|<big|int>Q<rsub|T><rsup|3>=<frac|1|16*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
    T+O<around|(|1|)>> for the heat profile. (b) Show that
    <math|Q<rsub|T><rsup|3>-<around|(|Q<rsub|T>-Q<rsub|t>|)><rsup|3>> is
    integrable uniformly in <math|T>, and deduce that the counterterm
    <eqref|eq:gammaT> makes the <math|:\<phi\><rsup|2>>: coefficient in
    <eqref|eq:second-order> converge. (c) Show that the remaining part
    <math|<big|iint>S<rsup|3>*<around|(|x,y|)>*<around|(|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>-\<phi\><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|2>>
    converges for smooth <math|\<phi\>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Power counting><label|ex:power-counting> Show that the superficial
    degree of divergence of a connected graph of
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|d>> with <math|n> vertices and <math|E>
    external legs is <math|\<omega\>=d-<frac|d-2|2>*E+<around|(|d-4|)>*n>.
    Deduce that in <math|d=3> the only divergent graphs with loops are the
    two-point graphs with <math|n\<leq\>2> (the tadpole and double tadpole,
    removed by Wick ordering, and the sunset) and vacuum graphs with
    <math|n\<leq\>3>.
  </exercise>

  <section|Dyson\USchwinger equations at every scale><label|sec:DS>

  Assume now that all the operators involved are functions of <math|A>, hence
  commute, and let

  <\equation*>
    K\<assign\>Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>J<rsub|t>\<assign\>K*Q<rsub|t>,<space|2em>J<rsub|0>=0,<space|1em>J<rsub|T>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1>.
  </equation*>

  For the heat profile, <math|K=A\<mathe\><rsup|A/T<rsup|2>>> and
  <math|J<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|-A*<around|(|t<rsup|-2>-T<rsup|-2>|)>>> is a
  heat semigroup, which tends to <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>> as
  <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>. <math|F<rsub|t>> is the force observable of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:fbsde>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|DS equations at resolution <math|t>><label|thm:DS-scale>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> The <em|noise observable>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:eta>\<eta\><rsub|t>\<assign\>K*\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>
    </equation>

    is a centred <math|\<bbb-P\>>-martingale, with
    <math|\<mathd\>\<eta\><rsub|t>=K*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>>.
    For every <math|t\<leq\>T>, every <math|h> and every functional <math|G>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:DS-scale>\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>>*<around*|[|D<rsub|J<rsub|t>*h>*G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|h,\<eta\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|h,K*\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>.
    </equation>

    At <math|t=T> this is the Dyson\USchwinger equation of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:DS-terminal>\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|T>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*G|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|T>><around*|[|G<space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|h,K*\<varphi\>+D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>|]>.
    </equation>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    From <eqref|eq:fbsde> and <math|<wide|J|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=K*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>,
    <math|\<mathd\>\<eta\><rsub|t>=K*<around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>\<mathd\>t+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>|)>-K*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>\<mathd\>t-J<rsub|t>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>>.
    The drift cancels, <math|F> is a martingale and <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>=0>.
    For <eqref|eq:DS-scale> use Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals>:
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|t>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>.
    Gaussian integration by parts for <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>> in the
    direction <math|Q<rsub|t>*k> gives <math|\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|t>>*<around|[|D<rsub|Q<rsub|t>*k>*G|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|t>>*<around|[|G*<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|k,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|Q<rsub|t>*k,D*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>|)>|]>>.
    Take <math|k=K*h>, so that <math|Q<rsub|t>*k=J<rsub|t>*h>, and recall
    <math|F<rsub|t>=-D*V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>.
  </proof>

  Formally letting <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|K\<to\>A>, and
  <eqref|eq:eta> becomes an exact identity between observables at every
  resolution:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:field-eq-scale>A*\<Phi\><rsub|t>=A*Q<rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>F<rsub|t>+\<eta\><rsub|t>,
  </equation>

  with <math|A*Q<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>> for the heat profile.
  This is the Euclidean field equation <math|A*\<varphi\>=-V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>+>noise
  of Lectures 1\U2, seen at resolution <math|t>. It needs no products of
  distributions: the nonlinearity enters only through the force
  <em|observable> <math|F<rsub|t>>, smeared at scale <math|1/t>. For the GFF
  (<math|F=0>), <eqref|eq:DS-scale> is Gaussian integration by parts for
  <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>. For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>, where
  <math|F<rsub|t>> plays the role of the renormalised cube, the limit
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> of <eqref|eq:field-eq-scale> corresponds to the
  renormalised DS equation of<nbsp><cite|GH21>.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Comparison with stochastic quantisation>In parabolic or elliptic
    stochastic quantisation (Lecture 2, Ÿ3.5\U3.6) the field equation is
    imposed in an auxiliary time, and the field at fixed time has the right
    law. Here the field equation <eqref|eq:field-eq-scale> holds at each
    resolution, and the auxiliary parameter is the resolution itself. Taking
    conditional expectations of an SPDE solution with respect to a scale
    filtration gives identities of the same shape, but with a different noise
    covariance: the martingale <math|J<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>>
    in <math|\<mathd\>\<eta\><rsub|t>> is essential<nbsp><cite|BCG26>.
  </remark>

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|DS from the dynamics>Prove <eqref|eq:DS-scale> without using
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals>, for <math|G> of the form
    <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> with <math|G<rsub|t>>
    solving <eqref|eq:kolmogorov>. Show that the drift of
    <math|D<rsub|J<rsub|t>*h>*G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> equals
    the covariation of <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> with
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|h,\<eta\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>>. Where is the
    symmetry of <math|D*F<rsub|t>> used?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Gaussian check>For <math|V<rsub|T>=0> show that
    <eqref|eq:DS-scale> reduces to <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|Q<rsub|t>*K*h>*G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)><around|\<langle\>|K*h,X<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>>,
    and compare with the DS characterisation of the GFF in Lecture 2.
  </exercise>

